<h2>Lead Nurturing Strategies: Convert the 90 Percent Who Are Not Ready to Buy Today</h2>

<p>The biggest leak in most solar marketing funnels is not lead generation. It is lead nurturing. Solar buyers take an average of 8.9 months from first interest to signed contract. During that time, they are bombarded with competitor ads, distracted by life events, and paralyzed by the complexity of the decision. A structured nurture system keeps your company top-of-mind and moves prospects toward a sale.</p>

<h3>The 7-Touch Solar Nurture Sequence</h3>

<p>A high-performing solar nurture sequence includes seven touchpoints over 45 days. Touch one (immediate): automated welcome email with a personalized savings estimate and clear next steps. Touch two (day 2): educational video explaining how solar works and what to expect during installation. Touch three (day 5): financing guide comparing cash purchase, solar loan, lease, and PPA options. Touch four (day 10): local case study featuring a customer in the prospect's neighborhood with specific savings numbers. Touch five (day 17): objection-handling content addressing roof condition, HOA approval, and utility interconnection. Touch six (day 25): limited-time offer or seasonal incentive reminder. Touch seven (day 45): re-engagement email with a new savings estimate or updated incentive information.</p>

<p>Each touchpoint should provide standalone value. If a prospect opens only one email, that email should still be useful. One installer using this sequence increased their lead-to-appointment rate from 9 percent to 18 percent, effectively doubling their pipeline without increasing ad spend.</p>

<h3>Multi-Channel Nurturing: Email, SMS, and Retargeting Combined</h3>

<p>Email alone is not enough. The most effective nurture systems combine three channels. Email delivers detailed educational content and proposals. SMS handles quick updates, appointment reminders, and urgent incentive deadlines. Retargeting ads keep your brand visible on Facebook, Instagram, and the Google Display Network. One solar company found that adding SMS to their email sequence increased response rates by 3x, particularly for evening sends between 7 and 9 PM.</p>

<p>The key is channel-specific content. Do not copy-paste your email into an SMS. An email can be 300 words with images and links. An SMS should be under 160 characters with a single call to action. A retargeting ad should be visual and attention-grabbing. Match the message to the medium.</p>

<h3>Lead Scoring: Know Who Is Hot and Who Is Cold</h3>

<p>Not all leads deserve the same attention. Lead scoring helps your sales team prioritize. Assign points for high-intent behaviors: visiting the pricing page (10 points), downloading a financing guide (15 points), watching a testimonial video (5 points), returning to the website three times in a week (20 points). When a lead hits a threshold, trigger an immediate alert to your sales team for personal outreach.</p>

<p>One California installer reduced their sales cycle from 4.5 months to 3.2 months by implementing automated lead scoring. Their sales reps stopped chasing cold leads and focused on prospects who had demonstrated clear buying signals. Revenue per sales rep increased 67 percent.</p>

